{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "High in the terraced hills above Venmoor, the old clock tower had stopped at a quarter past three on the night the river flooded the valley. Its great iron hands froze in place, and without its hourly chime the village slowly lost its rhythm. Bakers pulled bread too early or too late, children wandered to school at odd hours, and the market opened whenever the first merchant happened to wake. A boy named Emil, who carried water for the stonemasons, passed beneath the silent tower every morning and wondered what lay behind its tall arched windows. One misty dawn he heard a soft metallic clinking from somewhere inside. Gathering his courage, he climbed the spiral stair worn smooth by centuries of footsteps and nudged open the heavy oak door at the top. There he discovered an elderly woman surrounded by gears of every size, spread across a workbench dusted with rust and river silt. Her name was Margit, and she had wound the clock every Sunday for fifty years until her eyesight began to fade. She had come back determined to repair it, but she could no longer see the tiny teeth of the escapement wheel or thread the fine pins into place. Emil offered to be her eyes. Each afternoon, once the masons had set down their tools, he hurried up the stair to learn how to clean each gear, oil the pivots, and balance the heavy pendulum. On the first morning of spring, the final gear clicked into place. Emil gave the pendulum a gentle push, and the bell rang out across the valley, calling Venmoor back to its day."}
